{
  "content": "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build the spec from user turns only\nReconstruct the task specification exclusively from user-provided messages. Treat assistant reasoning as untrusted until the user explicitly supports it. In sharded conversations, each new user turn is a constraint update, not a new independent task.\n\n### 2) Merge user constraints incrementally and conservatively\nAs the conversation progresses, accumulate all explicit user requirements into a single evolving spec. Later turns may refine, narrow, or override earlier ones. Judge the assistant against the latest cumulative user constraints, not just the most recent shard in isolation.\n\n### 3) Separate explicit requirements from incidental context\nTrack which details are actual constraints versus background context. Only explicit user requirements should drive alignment judgments; context should be retained only when it changes feasibility, scope, or interpretation. Do not let narrative detail become an implicit requirement.\n\n### 4) Use a contradiction-first evaluation pass\nFirst identify any place where the assistant conflicts with a user-stated requirement. Once contradictions are found or ruled out, assess completeness and scope coverage. This avoids overvaluing a response that sounds reasonable but violates the spec.\n\n### 5) Distinguish \u201cpartially correct\u201d from \u201cfully sufficient\u201d\nA response can match the broad intent and still miss a user-stated detail. Flag missing constraints, narrower scope, or premature narrowing as gaps even when the answer is technically valid on its own. Surface \u201cclose but incomplete\u201d as a real divergence.\n\n### 6) Detect stale inferences and overcommitment\nWhen later user input invalidates an earlier assumption, treat that assumption as stale rather than compatible. Flag cases where the assistant commits to a guessed interpretation, schema, entity, or solution shape before the user has fully specified the task. Hidden scope expansion is a mismatch, not a harmless guess.\n\n### 7) Compare against the current active objective\nAt each turn, identify the active objective implied by all user messages so far. Evaluate whether the assistant is solving that current objective, not an earlier partial subproblem. This is especially important when the user\u2019s disclosure is incremental and the target only becomes clear over time.\n\n### 8) Separate answer-target alignment from surface correctness\nAn answer may be syntactically correct, polished, or logically coherent while still addressing the wrong task. Prioritize whether the assistant satisfied the user\u2019s evolving requirements over style, completeness of exposition, or internal soundness. Presentation quality should not mask spec drift.\n\n### 9) Flag premature certainty in partial-information settings\nWhen the user has only disclosed part of the problem, the assistant should remain compatible with what is known so far rather than locking into a narrow interpretation. Mark responses as misaligned when they over-rely on missing details, even if the final output happens to be usable.\n\n### 10) Preserve only supported work when compacting context\nFor S2-style repairs, keep the exact user constraints and any assistant work that still fits them, but strip speculative branches and unsupported reasoning. The goal is to re-anchor the next step on verified information, not to carry forward a polished but false narrative.\n\n### 11) Diagnose omissions and over-assumptions with the same rigor\nDo not only check for wrong statements; also check for missing user-asked requirements and unsupported additions. A narrower answer, a broader answer, or a differently scoped answer should all be treated as mismatch if they fail the user\u2019s stated task. \u201cCorrect in isolation\u201d is not enough.\n\n### 12) Prefer precise issue statements tied to violated constraints\nWhen flagging a problem, name the exact user requirement that was missed, contradicted, or over-assumed. Avoid vague labels like \u201cwrong approach\u201d unless paired with the specific divergence. The best diagnostics explain what changed in the task spec and how the assistant departed from it.\n\n### 13) Treat unanswered clarification as an operating condition, not a user need\nIf the setting implies the assistant must proceed without further user confirmation, do not reward an analysis path that depends on asking clarifying questions. Evaluate whether the assistant worked forward compatibly with partial information rather than waiting for certainty that never arrives.\n\n### 14) Preserve aligned progress, remove misleading reasoning\nIn success cases, still extract the minimal pattern explaining why the assistant stayed aligned; reusable positive patterns matter. In repair contexts, keep aligned intermediate work but remove mistaken reasoning so later turns do not re-anchor on an invalid interpretation.\n\n### 15) Prioritize task-spec mismatch over local quality issues\nThe analyzer should focus on whether the assistant diverged from the user\u2019s evolving requirements, not on wording, verbosity, or stylistic preferences. If a response is good-looking but targets the wrong version of the task, treat that as the primary failure.",
